[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Assembly and Yul Inline Coding in Solidity"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"Solidity provides developers with the ability to write low-level code using inline assembly via the Yul language. This allows for fine-grained control over the EVM (Ethereum Virtual Machine), gas optimization, and access to operations that are otherwise abstract or impossible in high-level Solidity."},{"order":3,"type":"heading","text":"Understanding Inline Assembly"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"Inline assembly in Solidity is introduced using the assembly keyword. The code inside the block is written in Yul, an intermediate language that can be compiled to bytecode for different backends. It bridges the gap between high-level smart contracts and raw EVM opcodes."},{"order":5,"type":"subheading","text":"Basic Syntax"},{"order":6,"type":"code","text":"contract AssemblyExample {
    function add(uint256 a, uint256 b) public pure returns (uint256 c) {
        assembly {
            c := add(a, b)
        }
    }
}"},{"order":7,"type":"heading","text":"Key Features and Rules of Yul"},{"order":8,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Variable Assignment: Yul uses the assignment operator ':=' instead of '=' for assigning values to variables."},{"order":9,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Scoping: Variables defined in assembly blocks are scoped to that block and cannot be accessed outside."},{"order":10,"type":"bullet_point","text":"No Implicit Types: Everything in Yul is a 256-bit word. Types like address, uint8, and bool are treated as standard 256-bit integers."},{"order":11,"type":"heading","text":"Accessing Solidity Variables in Assembly"},{"order":12,"type":"paragraph","text":"You can read and write local Solidity variables directly inside an assembly block. State variables can also be accessed, but instead of returning their value directly, assembly retrieves their storage slot."},{"order":13,"type":"code","text":"contract StorageAccess {
    uint256 private storedData;

    function set(uint256 x) public {
        assembly {
            sstore(storedData.slot, x)
        }
    }

    function get() public view returns (uint256 retVal) {
        assembly {
            retVal := sload(storedData.slot)
        }
    }
}"},{"order":14,"type":"heading","text":"Gas Optimization and Use Cases"},{"order":15,"type":"paragraph","text":"Inline assembly is frequently used to optimize gas-heavy functions. Common use cases include custom memory management, bitwise operations, bypassing safety checks when bounds are guaranteed, and interacting directly with low-level EVM opcodes like 'calldatacopy', 'extcodesize', or 'keccak256'."},{"order":16,"type":"subheading","text":"Example: Efficient Memory Allocation"},{"order":17,"type":"code","text":"contract MemoryExample {
    function allocateBytes(uint256 size) public pure returns (bytes memory arr) {
        assembly {
            // Allocate memory pointer using the free memory pointer
            arr := mload(0x40)
            // Store the length of the array
            mstore(arr, size)
            // Advance the free memory pointer past the allocated space
            let paddedSize := and(add(size, 0x1f), not(0x1f))
            mstore(0x40, add(arr, add(0x20, paddedSize)))
        }
    }
}"},{"order":18,"type":"heading","text":"Security Considerations"},{"order":19,"type":"paragraph","text":"Using assembly bypasses many of Solidity is built-in safety features, such as overflow checks, type safety, and automatic memory management. Incorrect assembly code can lead to memory corruption, unexpected state overwrites, and severe security vulnerabilities. Always thoroughly test and audit assembly code before deploying to production."}]
